ource state |\\n152:| A8 | Detaching a source attachment removes it from current active use but does not erase prior process history or prior durable code-update visibility | Validated | Product + Platform | Current work and prior provenance remain distinct concerns |\\n154:| A10 | Attach, update, refresh, and detach use request/response behavior in the existing shell and process surfaces rather than through a separate source-management live subscription | Validated | Platform | Refresh may either settle immediately or return a durable pending refresh state when the work cannot complete cheaply; Epic 6 does not add a new live-update surface |\\n179:provenance.\\n193:- update, refresh, and detach act on the durable `sourceAttachmentId`\\n203:  shadows the project-scoped attachment for that one process's current-source\\n207:  is the normalized GitHub identity used for uniqueness and provenance\\n402:In Epic 6, source provenance is durable, process-specific, and complements the\\n403:artifact-version provenance aligned in Epic 5. Epic 6 requires durable\\n404:process-specific provenance records; the exact schema is deferred to Tech\\n409:Epic 6 records provenance durably at two moments:\\n411:- `informed_work` provenance is recorded when process work uses attached sources\\n413:- `received_code_update` provenance is recorded when a durable code update lands\\n416:Provenance remains durable after detach because each provenance entry copies\\n422:Each provenance relationship resolves independently. If one provenance lookup\\n423:cannot fully enrich current attachment context, the provenance endpoint still\\n424:returns the remaining entries and a bounded degraded entry for the failing\\n426:that provenance record.\\n430:**AC-4.1:** The user can tell which attached repositories and refs informed the\\n433:- **TC-4.1a: Informing source provenance visible**\\n435:  - When: User views current source provenance\\n437:- **TC-4.1b: Empty provenance state shown for process with no recorded source use**\\n438:  - Given: A process has no recorded source provenance yet\\n439:  - When: User views current source provenance\\n440:  - Then: The platform shows a clear empty provenance state\\n442:**AC-4.2:** The user can tell which attached writable repository and ref\\n445:- **TC-4.2a: Receiving source provenance visible**\\n447:  - When: User views current source provenance\\n450:**AC-4.3:** Read-only source attachments never appear as if they received\\n453:- **TC-4.3a: Read-only source not shown as write target**\\n455:  - When: User views source provenance after code work\\n458:**AC-4.4:** One failing provenance lookup does not fail the rest of the\\n459:provenance surface.\\n461:- **TC-4.4a: One degraded provenance entry does not hide healthy entries**\\n462:  - Given: A process has multiple provenance relationships and one cannot fully\\n464:  - When: User loads process source provenance\\n465:  - Then: The healthy provenance entries remain visible and the failing one is\\n466:    returned as a bounded degraded entry\\n467:- **TC-4.4b: Degraded provenance entry falls back to durable identity**\\n468:  - Given: One provenance lookup cannot enrich current attachment metadata\\n469:  - When: The provenance surface loads\\n470:  - Then: The degraded entry still shows the durable repository identity and\\n479:If a running process already has a detached source in its hydrated working copy,\\n480:detach removes that source from future current attachment state but does not\\n482:that still depends on the detached source follows the existing checkpoint\\n484:The detach operation is soft detach: it marks the attachment detached or\\n485:inactive for current use, such as with `detachedAt`, rather than hard-deleting\\n486:identity needed for provenance.\\n489:2. User chooses to detach it from current project or process use\\n491:4. System preserves prior visible provenance and prior process history\\n496:**AC-5.1:** The user can detach a source attachment from current project or\\n499:- **TC-5.1a: Detach project-scoped source**\\n501:  - When: User detaches it\\n503:- **TC-5.1b: Detach process-scoped source**\\n505:  - When: User detaches it\\n507:- **TC-5.1c: Detach during active process work does not rewrite the current hydrated copy**\\n509:  - When: User detaches that source from current use\\n512:**AC-5.2:** Detaching a source attachment does not erase prior visible\\n513:provenance or prior process history that already referenced that source.\\n515:- **TC-5.2a: Prior provenance remains after detach**\\n517:  - When: User detaches that source attachment from current use\\n518:  - Then: Prior visible provenance and prior process history remain available\\n520:**AC-5.3:** Detaching one source attachment does not remove unrelated current\\n523:- **TC-5.3a: Unrelated attachments remain**\\n525:  - When: User detaches one source attachment\\n544:**AC-6.1:** Reloading or reopening the project shell or process work surface\\n547:- **TC-6.1a: Reopen project source attachment state**\\n551:- **TC-6.1b: Reopen process source attachment state**\\n556:**AC-6.2:** If a requested source attachment is unavailable or access is\\n560:- **TC-6.2a: Unavailable source attachment shown safely**\\n564:- **TC-6.2b: Revoked access blocks source management**\\n569:**AC-6.3:** If one source attachment fails independently, the rest of the\\n572:- **TC-6.3a: One failing source does not hide healthy sources**\\n588:| `/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}` | Process work surface showing current sources for that process, including process-scoped sources and applicable project-scoped sources after shadowing/conflict rules, plus source provenance |\\n604:| Get process source provenance | GET | `/api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}/source-provenance` | Returns process-specific provenance entries for attached sources that informed or received work, complementing artifact-version provenance from Epic 5; one degraded provenance lookup does not fail the whole read |\\n612:| repositoryFullName | string | no | `owner/name` format when present | Normalized canonical GitHub identity used for uniqueness and provenance; may be provided by the client or derived from `repositoryUrl` during validation |\\n641:| repositoryFullName | string | yes | `owner/name` format, non-empty | Normalized canonical GitHub identity used for uniqueness, conflict handling, and provenance; new in Epic 6 |\\n652:| detachedAt | string | no | ISO 8601 UTC when present | Present only for responses that need to describe a soft-detached attachment; detached attachments are excluded from current active source lists |\\n662:| provenanceId | string | yes | non-empty | Stable provenance identifier |\\n663:| sourceAttachmentId | string | no | non-empty when present | Attached source this provenance entry refers to, when the source attachment is still available |\\n666:| repositoryUrl | string | yes | full GitHub URL, non-empty | Operational source URL copied at the time provenance is recorded |\\n668:| entryStatus | enum | yes | `ready` or `degraded` | Whether this provenance entry resolved cleanly or is returned with bounded degradation |\\n669:| degradationReason | string | no | non-empty when present | Why this provenance entry is degraded when `entryStatus` is `degraded` |\\n670:| recordedAt | string | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Time this provenance relationship was recorded |\\n672:These entries complement the artifact version provenance defined in Epic 5.\\n692:| refreshStatus | enum | yes | `settled`, `pending`, or `failed` | Whether the refresh settled in the request, was accepted for longer-running work, or failed |\\n699:| detached | boolean | yes | `true` | Indicates the source attachment was removed from current active use |\\n701:| detachedAt | string | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Time the attachment was soft-detached from current active use |\\n707:| entries | array of Source Provenance Entry | yes | present | Process-specific provenance entries for attached sources that informed or received work |\\n711:A successful provenance read may contain both `ready` and `degraded` entries.\\n712:Per-entry provenance degradation does not force a request-level error when the\\n713:rest of the provenance surface is still readable.\\n717:In Epic 6, attach, update, refresh, and detach are request/response actions\\n719:source-management live subscription. Attach, update, and detach settle in the\\n725:allowed. `refreshStatus: failed` means the refresh was accepted but resolved\\n751:  references, and review/package provenance rules\\n791:- Detaching a source does not erase prior visible provenance or prior process\\n795:- One degraded provenance lookup does not fail the rest of the provenance\\n802:- Source attach, update, refresh, and detach actions are keyboard reachable\\n809:- Source attach, update, refresh, detach, and failure events are logged with\\n821:   detach sources inside the existing project and process surfaces without\\n824:   source attachments, including canonical repository identity and provenance\\n834:5. What exact provenance surface should show informed-work relationships and\\n836:   artifact-version provenance from Epic 5?\\n841:   project-scoped and process-scoped sources, apply process-scoped shadowing,\\n843:8. What soft-detach representation should preserve source identity and\\n844:   provenance visibility while excluding detached attachments from current\\n852:this epic owns artifact-model migration or review/package provenance\\n858:repository identity contracts, provenance shapes, error classes, hydration and\\n859:freshness fixtures for the four-state model, Epic 5-aligned material/provenance\\n900:received process work, and the provenance surface degrades one failing lookup\\n905:- AC-4.1 (informing source provenance visible)\\n906:- AC-4.2 (receiving source provenance visible)\\n907:- AC-4.3 (read-only source not shown as write target)\\n908:- AC-4.4 (one failing provenance lookup degrades independently)\\n917:- AC-5.1 (detach project-scoped or process-scoped source)\\n918:- AC-5.2 (prior provenance remains after detach)\\n919:- AC-5.3 (unrelated attachments remain)\\n928:- AC-6.1 (reopen project/process source attachment state)\\n929:- AC-6.2 (unavailable or revoked source-management state)\\n930:- AC-6.3 (one failing source does not hide healthy sources)\\n937:- [ ] Flows cover happy, alternate, refresh, detach, and degraded-state paths\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
